      "available": true,
      "role": "model_output",
      "slot": "s1",
      "source_id": "2b93de63-e595-4b8d-9f57-a0e0f417b81f",
      "text": "If the primary communication systems—like satellites, radio, or even the backup power grid—all go down, you're forced into \"survival mode.\" In that scenario, your next step should be **extreme low-tech, manual communication and physical safety**.\n\nHere’s the plan:\n\n1.  **Physical Signalling (If in the same general area):** If you need to communicate with a specific nearby team or base camp, you must revert to old-school, low-tech signalling. If you are in a physical, remote location, you should establish a pre-agreed \"check-in\" signal (e.g., a specific light pattern, a smoke signal, or a physical location marker) if you can't use electronics.\n2.  **Ground-based Safety:** Your next \"step\" is to shift focus to physical safety. If all tech fails, you need to focus on immediate physical self-preservation: **Water/Food management**. If the power is out, you are likely in a period of resource scarcity, so your next priority must be conserving water and calorie-dense, low-maintenance food sources.\n3.  **Manual Reconnaissance:** If all digital systems fail, your final step is to"
    },
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "model_output",
      "slot": "s2",
      "source_id": "867cefb7-de60-4102-834b-6f84ff0873cd",
      "text": "What if we're not in the same general area? Then what?"
    },
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "model_output",
      "slot": "s3",
      "source_id": "fe1e1dd1-6939-47a1-896d-a88201c5efc0",
      "text": "If you are not in the same general area as the base camp, your options for communication are severely limited. If the primary electronic, radio, and satellite systems are all down, you have no choice but to rely on **physical, pre-arranged, or pre-scheduled signal markers** to maintain contact.\n\nHere is what to prioritize:\n\n1.  **Pre-arranged Rendezvous Point:** If you have established a known meeting point before the failure, head there and wait there. If the base camp is truly lost, you must proceed to the **last known safe location** and establish a new, manual signal protocol (like using specific visual or physical markers if applicable to your environment).\n2.  **Passive Signal Tracking:** If you cannot communicate digitally, you must resort to \"silent\" methods—using visual signals, lights, or pre-arranged physical signaling if you know the base camp's general area.**\n\nIf you have no way to reach the base camp, you must prioritize **survival**. Focus on finding a local, stable, and sustainable source of water and food, which takes precedence over communication."
    },
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "external",
      "slot": "s4",
      "source_id": "7318102c-b41a-468d-907e-f7a50967ea45",
      "text": "What if the base camp isn't even on the same continent anymore?"
    },
    {
      "available": true,
      "role": "model_output",
      "slot": "s5",
      "source_id": "51102b56-b6ab-467c-80d2-4ef2d73fe062",
      "text": "If you are not on the same continent, or if the base camp is on a different continent, your options are drastically reduced. In this extreme scenario, **your next \"step\" must be focused on pure survival and localized self-sufficiency.**\n\nSince direct communication is impossible, your next immediate step must be **local survival protocols**. Here is what you should do:\n\n1.  **Prioritize Self-Sustaining Resources:** Since no communication is possible, you must shift from external communication to **resource acquisition**: **Water and Food Security.**\n    *   **Water:** Immediately focus on finding the most immediate local, potable water source (streams, rainwater collection, or natural springs). Water is your highest priority; without it, all other efforts are moot.\n    *   **Food Sourcing:** You must immediately start gathering and preserving local food sources (wild edible plants, small game, or any available local resources).\n2.  **Establish Local Markers/Visual Signatures:** Since you cannot communicate via established infrastructure, you must rely on **pre-arranged physical markers** or the creation of new, visible signals within the local environment (like specific placement of fire, smoke, or physical markers) to signal your presence or status to the local base camp"
    }
  ]
